WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:04.660
So today we’re going to look at bromine.
So this is bromine, it’s a small

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:08.780
sample, about 2 or 3 grammes.

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:14.460
Bromine is a red liquid. There are not many
elements that are actually

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:21.109
liquid: caesium, mercury, gallium and bromine.
It’s this beautiful red

00:00:21.109 --> 00:00:27.210
colour and it has an unpleasant smell. Bromine
comes from the Greek

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:30.460
word bromos which means stench, a really horrible
smell.

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:34.629
In fact this bromine probably comes from near
where I did my degree

00:00:34.629 --> 00:00:39.079
because in Europe, the second, well the largest
producer of bromine in

00:00:39.079 --> 00:00:42.050
Europe is the Octale Company which is based
on Amlwch on Anglesey

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:46.910
which is where I went last weekend. So it’s
a nice sample. We’re going to

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:49.469
get it out of this bottle now because it’s
sealed in because it’s obviously

00:00:49.469 --> 00:00:55.320
very, very volatile as a liquid and we’re
going to cut the glass, get it out

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:56.320
and look at its chemistry. Thanks Neil.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:02.780
Bromine is also quite a common element in
the world. It is found in sea

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:10.190
water as bromide, it’s also found particularly
in the Dead Sea between

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:15.370
Israel and Jordan where there are very concentrated
solutions of

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:21.900
bromide. So you can make bromine just by bubbling
chlorine into a

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:27.930
solution of bromide like Dead Sea water and
the chlorine displaces the

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:31.820
bromine and forms chloride and the bromine
just comes out as red

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:34.110
fumes which you can catch.

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:38.060
So Neil’s now using a glass knife to open
the ampoule and he’s going to

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:42.350
pour the liquid bromine into this small evaporating
dish inside our fume

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:46.820
hood. Now instantly you can see the vapour
of the bromine coming off.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:50.500
You see all those really nice orange fumes
which are coming off from the

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:54.040
liquid and then they’re being drawn away
by our fume hood.

00:01:54.040 --> 00:02:01.900
Bromine has a number of uses, it is particularly
used in plastics as an

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:08.200
additive to try and make them less flammable.
If you’re watching your

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:13.700
videos on a screen which has a plastic surround
almost certainly that

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:19.920
surround will contain bromine to make it less
flammable.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:23.090
Bromine itself is strongly oxidising so we
thought we’d do an oxidation

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:26.350
reaction today. And that reaction is to take
another element and here we

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:30.040
have aluminium, simple aluminium foil, like
you wrap your dinner in, and

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:34.850
we’re now going to put some small amounts
of aluminium foil into the

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:40.730
bromine liquid. So we put it in and it generally
takes a few moments for

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:44.750
this reaction to start but as I said, the
aluminium is strongly oxidising. It

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:50.150
oxidises and generates aluminium tribromide.
Which is a beautiful nice

00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:53.100
material but it’s a strongly exothermic
reaction. So let’s see what

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:55.720
happens. This may initiate.

00:02:55.720 --> 00:03:03.210
It is also an unusual element because elements
have different isotopes

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:07.240
these are the same form of the element but
with different numbers of

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:14.640
neutrons in the atoms so they weigh different
amounts. Usually you get

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:18.890
one isotope that is very common and another
one that is only a small

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:24.940
amount. Bromine is unusual because it has
two isotopes 79 and 81 which

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:30.650
have almost equal quantities in nature so
that they have approximately

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:35.910
51% of one and 49% of the other which is really
quite unusual.

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:42.150
So here we see the exothermic oxidation and
formation of aluminium

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:51.041
tribromide.
So the reaction is strongly exothermic which

00:03:51.041 --> 00:03:54.690
means it gives
out energy and that energy then evaporates

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:58.630
off excess bromine which
you can see coming off as a vapour. A wonderful

00:03:58.630 --> 00:04:01.930
exothermic reaction:
very, very fast, very, very rapid forming

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:18.579
aluminium tribromide.

00:04:18.579 --> 00:04:22.500
Bromine is quite dangerous but it is less
dangerous than chlorine because

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:26.870
it’s a liquid at room temperature. Chlorine
is a gas so if you let out

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:32.040
chlorine it disperses everywhere. With bromine
it is a heavy gas which

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:41.680
can disperse rather more slowly.

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:44.710
So now as the reaction cools down we can see
that the volume of

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:46.120
bromine is significantly reduced.

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:46.340
Reacts with water quite well.

